    "id": "339836930",
    "body": "**Flagging a discrepancy found while reviewing [Proposed: Tag-Based Environment Promotion (GitFlow)](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/335216656) (finding raised by Marcel Navarro, verified against the live workflow files).**\n\nThe **GitHub Environment secret scope** table on this page lists `staging`, `sit`, `pre-prod`, `uat`, `production`. The real GitHub Environments bound in `.github/workflows/deploy-*.yml` today are `dev`, `prod`, `sit`, `staging`, `test`, `uat` — confirmed directly from each workflow's `environment:` key. `pre-prod` and `production` don't exist as GitHub Environment names in this repo, and `dev`/`test` aren't in the table at all (`test` is a newer tier — `deploy-test.yml` — that likely postdates this page).\n\nThere's a second, more load-bearing issue underneath the naming mismatch: this page's own Tier 2 (DEV) row labels its MRT target \"MRT staging,\" and the secret-scope table lists `staging` as holding **Dev credentials, no reviewer required**. But `deploy-stg.yml` — the actual STAGE-tier deploy workflow — binds `environment: staging` in the repo today, not `deploy-dev.yml`. So the GitHub Environment literally named `staging` is gated to the STAGE tier's deploy, not DEV's, which is the opposite of what this table implies. Since \"reviewer required\" is a real access-control setting per GitHub Environment, an incorrect DEV/no-reviewer association against what's actually the STAGE-tier environment is a live discrepancy worth reconciling, not just a docs nit — this is the same stg/staging confusion already called out in `deploy-stg.yml`'s own fix history.\n\n**Suggest:** reconcile this table's environment names and reviewer-requirement column against the actual `environment:` keys in `.github/workflows/deploy-*.yml`, and add the `test` tier. Flagging rather than editing directly since this page is Integration/SI Partner-owned.",
