{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "305102849",
    "title": "Human-in-the-Lead AI Development — Project Setup Overview",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/305102849",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 3,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "**Project:** Zimmermann SFCC PWA Kit — Magento 2 → Salesforce Commerce Cloud **SI Partner:** Accenture **Architecture:** Slim overrides project on `@salesforce/retail-react-app` v10 (not a monorepo fork) **Period covered:** Pre-development setup phase, May 2026\n\nThe Approach: Human-in-the-Lead AI Development\n----------------------------------------------\n\nThis project was set up using a deliberate human-in-the-lead AI development model. That phrase describes something specific: **every architectural decision, every rule, and every governance boundary was made by a human.** AI was used to accelerate the execution of those decisions, propose options for the human to choose between, and implement approved plans — not to make design choices independently.\n\nThis distinction matters for leadership. The project is not \"AI-built\" — it is human-directed and AI-accelerated. The humans on this project defined what quality means, decided what was protected, chose the environment topology, and approved each implementation before it was committed.\n\nExecutive Summary\n-----------------\n\nBefore the first line of Zimmermann storefront code was written, a complete development infrastructure was designed and implemented through a structured collaboration between the project lead and two AI tools:\n\n**Stage 1 — Strategy and planning with Claude.ai.** Architecture decisions, environment strategies, and QA frameworks were discussed iteratively. Claude.ai proposed options with trade-offs; the project lead made the decisions. Outcomes were captured in planning documents (repo: `.docs/prompts/`) — these represent human decisions, not AI decisions.\n\n**Stage 2 — Review, correction and implementation with Claude Code CLI.** The planning documents were handed to Claude Code as implementation briefs. Claude Code first reviewed each document, identified errors and gaps specific to this project's structure, and flagged them for human review. After corrections were agreed, Claude Code implemented each item — always within a governance framework requiring human confirmation before significant changes.\n\n**What was built:** a production-ready development framework covering AI governance, quality gates, CI/CD pipelines, QA automation foundations, environment strategy, onboarding processes, branching rules, and team documentation — all in place before sprint 1 begins.\n\nPart 1 — The Planning Documents (Claude.ai Collaboration)\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\nStrategy documents created through Claude.ai conversations are stored in the repo at `.docs/prompts/` — the record of human decisions made during the planning phase, with Claude.ai acting as a knowledgeable thinking partner.\n\nEach planning document followed the same pattern: the project lead brought a problem or requirement to a Claude.ai session → Claude.ai proposed options, explained trade-offs, and asked clarifying questions one at a time, waiting for answers → the project lead made the decisions → Claude.ai drafted the document reflecting those decisions → the project lead reviewed, corrected, and approved the document → the approved document was handed to Claude Code as an implementation brief.\n\n**`.docs/prompts/qaClaudeCodeSetup.md` — QA Automation Framework.** The human decision: AI drafts tests, Lead QA validates them in plain English before any code is generated. Claude Code reviewed the planning document and caught implementation errors before they became committed code: the wrong test framework was referenced (corrected before implementation began), directory paths did not match the actual project structure (corrected), and the BrowserStack configuration needed to target only Zimmermann tests to avoid running the full upstream Salesforce suite.\n\nWhat was captured: Gherkin `.feature` files as source of truth (Lead QA must validate before Playwright specs are generated); Page Object Model with selectors living only in `e2e/support/pages/`; multi-region fixtures region-aware from day one (7 storefronts across 6 regions; US confirmed as a single site, REG-005); Magento baseline parity tests annotated `# DELTA:` on failure — never silently updated.\n\nThe Digital SIT quality gate and SIT environment strategy were defined in similar Claude.ai sessions — reflected in the `digital-sit.yml` CI workflow and the [05.6 - Environment Details](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/285540356) page.\n\nPart 2 — AI Governance Layer (AGENTS.md + .claude/)\n---------------------------------------------------\n\nThe most significant infrastructure investment was a comprehensive governance layer controlling precisely how Claude Code behaves on this project. Every rule was decided by a human and implemented by Claude Code under human direction. Central principle: **AI tools operate within explicit boundaries set by humans, not at their own discretion.**\n\n**AGENTS.md — the project rulebook** (with `CLAUDE.md` as a one-line stub). Every rule was a human decision — Claude.ai proposed options and explained trade-offs, the project lead chose. Contains: a Branch Rules table (which branches Claude Code can operate on and under what conditions); 14 Hard Rules — absolute prohibitions encoding team agreements (never modify design tokens, never rename shared hooks, never operate on protected branches, never use `--dangerously-skip-permissions` on shared branches, never reference later-sprint code); a Release Branch Behaviour section (supervised mode: state a plan, assess blast radius, wait for confirmation before every edit); a \"When to Stop and Ask a Human\" table; a Before-You-Run Checklist; and a Contact Map.\n\n**`.claude/rules/` — tiered file protection.** Tier 1 (Frozen, owner approval required before any change): design tokens (owner: UI Designer), Commerce SDK hooks (owner: Integration Lead), runtime config, CI/CD workflows. Tier 2 (Protected, second reviewer on PR required): global shell components, shared product components, shared commerce components, shared hooks with multiple consumers. Squad scope: even in squad-owned areas, only the owning squad should be prompting Claude Code there — Squad A owns checkout/cart/payments, Squad B owns PDP/PLP/search/account.\n\n**`.claude/hooks/` — automated checkpoints.** A branch-lock hook (in `.claude/settings.json`) physically blocks any Claude Code write when the current branch is `develop` or `main`. `tier2Warn.sh` surfaces a warning before editing a Tier 2 file. `contentfulDriftWarn.sh` checks for Contentful schema drift after a file edit. `testFileRemind.sh` reminds the developer to add a test/story after creating a new file.\n\n**`.claude/settings.json` — the permission model.** Reads freely (`git status`/`diff`/`log`, `find`, `grep`, `ls` — observation never requires approval); every write, edit, push, install, or destructive command requires approval; pre-hooks (branch lock + Tier 2 warning) and post-hooks (drift check + test reminder) fire automatically.\n\n**`.docs/memory/` — living project memory.** Five auto-maintained files give every Claude Code session current context before it acts: `architecture.md`, `projectState.md`, `feedback.md`, `references.md`, `userProfile.md`. Read at the start of every session via `/session-start`.\n\n**`.claude/skills/`** — human-directed workflow skills, invoked with a `/` prefix. Every skill was designed in collaboration with Claude.ai, reviewed by the project lead, and implemented by Claude Code. See the [Claude Code Skills Reference](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/297959425) page for the full reference — usage syntax, arguments, and each skill's human-in-the-lead checkpoint.\n\nPart 3 — Multi-IDE Support: Governance Independent of Tool Choice\n-----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n`AGENTS.md` + `.claude/rules/` is the single source of truth for AI rules. A sync script propagates the same rules to every other AI tool's expected config location, and a pre-commit hook blocks direct edits to the derived files so the source of truth stays uncontested.\n\n**`.cursor/rules/` — Cursor AI.** Governance rules mirrored to Cursor's `.mdc` rule format.\n\n**`.github/copilot-instructions.md` and `.vscode/instructions/` — GitHub Copilot / VS Code.** Copilot instruction files mirror the tier rules, QA model, branching conventions, and coding standards.\n\nWhether a developer uses Claude Code, Cursor, or GitHub Copilot, they operate under the same human-defined boundaries — the governance layer is tool-agnostic.\n\nPart 4 — CI/CD Pipeline: Automated Quality with Human Release Authority\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**`digital-sit.yml` — the Digital SIT Gate.** The primary quality gate workflow, running automatically on every `develop` push and every PR to `release/**`: install → playwright-p0, lighthouse-ci, contract-tests, bundle-size → sit-gate → deploy-sit (auto after sit-gate passes on `develop` pushes) → tag-sit-deploy → tag-sprint (when `scripts/sprint.txt` changes). Key human-in-the-lead decisions: the `sit-gate` job uses `if: always()` so branch protection always has a signal to check even when upstream jobs fail; SIT only receives code that has passed the Digital SIT gate; sprint tags require a human to increment `scripts/sprint.txt` — the system does not decide when a sprint ends.\n\n**`qa.yml` — cross-browser QA.** Unit tests run on every PR for fast feedback; BrowserStack E2E (Chrome, Safari, Firefox, Edge) runs only on PRs to `release/**`, where the stakes are higher. Keeping this separate from `digital-sit.yml` was a deliberate choice — mixing cross-browser tests into the main gate would slow every `develop` push.\n\n**`protected-file-guard.yml` — Tier 1 CI enforcement.** A CI-level complement to CODEOWNERS: when a PR touches a Tier 1 file, this workflow automatically requests review from the file owner, who must approve before the PR can merge.\n\n**`upstream-release-watch.yml` — upstream sync monitoring.** Watches the upstream Salesforce PWA Kit repo for new releases and notifies the team so a human can decide whether to bump and assess the impact on Zimmermann customisations. Policy: repo `.docs/upstreamPolicy.md`.\n\nFull CI/CD reference (trigger matrix, job dependency graph, branch protection configuration): see [PWA CI/CD Pipeline (GitHub Actions)](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/305561601).\n\nPart 5 — QA Automation Framework: The Human-in-the-Lead QA Model\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDesigned specifically to prevent the most common failure mode in AI-assisted QA: **technically correct tests that test the wrong behaviour.** AI tools are very good at generating valid Playwright code; they are less reliable at judging whether the scenarios are the right ones — those judgements require a human.\n\n**The three-stage model:** Stage 1 — Claude Code drafts the Gherkin feature file from a Jira story and acceptance criteria, then stops. Stage 2 — Lead QA reads and validates the feature file, annotating corrections directly with `# QA ANNOTATION:` comments (are the scenarios complete? do the steps reflect actual user behaviour? are the right journeys tagged `@p0`?). Stage 3 — only after Lead QA sign-off does Claude Code generate the Playwright spec. The feature file is the spec; the Playwright code is the execution.\n\n**Three testing layers — additive, not overlapping:** Vitest (unit logic — data mapping, integration boundaries, complex state), Storybook `play` functions (component interaction), Playwright (full storefront user journeys — the E2E layer covered above).\n\n**`e2e/features/`** — Gherkin as source of truth, naming `{JIRA-KEY}_{description}.feature`, required tags, P0 criteria. The P0 tag flows through to the Digital SIT gate. Magento 2 baseline parity tests are annotated `# DELTA:` on failure — brought to standup for human classification, never silently updated to pass.\n\n**`e2e/fixtures/`** — multi-region test data built in from day one: AU, US, UK, EU, ME, HK. The trade-off (one day of setup vs. weeks of rework) was discussed in the Claude.ai planning conversation and the project lead chose multi-region from the start.\n\n**`e2e/support/`** — Page Object Model; selectors live only in Page Object files, never in test files — a deliberate structural decision so that when Zimmermann's markup changes, selector updates happen in one place only.\n\nFull engineering reference for this layer: see [Guide 4: E2E Testing — Playwright & Gherkin Repo Conventions](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/305528833).\n\nPart 6 — Environment Infrastructure\n-----------------------------------\n\nAll environment infrastructure was agreed in the Claude.ai planning sessions and implemented by Claude Code with human review at each step.\n\n| Environment | Purpose | Deployed by |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Local | Feature dev on developer machines — all services mocked via MSW | `npm start` |\n| Dev | Integrated digital testing across all 7 storefronts | CI on every `develop` merge |\n| SIT | NewStore order management parallel lane — never gates Digital | CI after SIT gate passes |\n| Stage | Convergence — Digital + SIT both confirmed; contractual perf environment | Manual (Jira-booked window) |\n| UAT | Business acceptance across all 6 regions | Manual after Stage sign-off |\n| Production | Live — AU, UK, US, EU, ME, HK; AU canary first | Manual, release manager gate |\n\nDetailed topology: see the [Environment Topology & Promotion Path](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304840708) page. `scripts/sprint.txt` is a single-line file containing the current sprint number — the CI sprint tagging design requires a human to explicitly mark a sprint boundary rather than inferring it from dates.\n\nPart 7 — Git Infrastructure: Conventions That Protect the Team\n--------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**`pre-commit` and `commit-msg` hooks** — wired up automatically by Husky on `npm install`. `pre-commit` warns on Contentful definition drift and blocks direct edits to derived AI-config files (`.cursor/rules/`, `.vscode/instructions/`, `.github/copilot-instructions.md`) when their `.claude/` source is not also staged. `commit-msg` enforces conventional commits format — a deliberate choice, since the changelog skill depends on conventional commit types to route messages to the right audience (developer release notes vs. client-facing summary).\n\n**CODEOWNERS** — maps the tier system to GitHub review requirements. When a PR touches a Tier 1 file, GitHub automatically requests the owner's review and the PR cannot merge without it — enforced by GitHub, not just documented in a README.\n\n**`coderabbit.yaml` — AI-assisted PR review (with human authority).** CodeRabbit provides AI-assisted PR review at \"assertive\" profile: conventional commits compliance, Tier 1 changes flagged with owner and contact channel, Gherkin feature file quality checks, test coverage requirements. CodeRabbit's findings are advisory — developers and reviewers make the final call.\n\nPart 8 — Onboarding & Documentation\n-----------------------------------\n\nDocumentation was written to serve two audiences: humans reading it directly, and skills that read it at runtime to restore session context.\n\n| Doc | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Claude Code CLI — Developer Onboarding Guide](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304775192) (06 User Guides → Claude Guides) | Developer-facing Claude Code primer — install, authenticate, rules before first session |\n| [Branching Guide / GitFlow](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304840733) (05.2) | Plain-English branching model with every *why* explained alongside the *what* |\n| [PWA CI/CD Pipeline (GitHub Actions)](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/305561601) (05.2) | Full CI/CD trigger matrix, job dependency graph, and branch protection rules |\n| [7.1c CI/CD & Environment Setup Checklist](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/305430530) (07 PMO) | Tracked one-time CI/CD and environment setup tasks with owner and status |\n| [SIT Quality Gates](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304840754) (05.4) | Every gate signal with thresholds and investigation guidance |\n| [Environment Topology & Promotion Path](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304840708) / [05.6 Environment Details](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/285540356) | Environment topology and per-environment credentials |\n| [Guide 4: E2E Testing — Playwright & Gherkin Repo Conventions](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/305528833) (05.4) | QA guide — Gherkin + Playwright workflow |\n| repo: `.docs/upstreamPolicy.md` | Upstream PWA Kit bump policy (read-the-changelog, bump opportunistically) |\n| repo: `README.md` | Repo homepage — environments, storefronts, new joiner path |\n\nThe Human-in-the-Lead Principles, Applied Project-Wide\n------------------------------------------------------\n\n**1. Humans make decisions; AI proposes options.** Every architectural choice — environment topology, versioning scheme, branch protection rules, tier classifications — was made by the project lead. Claude.ai presented options and trade-offs. When a decision was deferred, it was saved to project memory with the options documented for the next conversation.\n\n**2. AI operates within human-defined boundaries.** The governance layer exists specifically to constrain AI behaviour so Claude Code only makes changes where humans have authorised it to.\n\n**3. Human approval gates at every high-consequence step.** QA: Lead QA validates Gherkin before tests are generated. Release: supervised mode requires plan + blast radius check + explicit confirmation before every edit. Tier 1 files: stop, name the file, confirm owner approval before proceeding. Branch protection: a named status check means a human configured the rule and the gate enforces it.\n\n**4. Humans can always override, recover, and correct.** The [Claude Code CLI Developer Onboarding Guide](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304775192) documents how to revert unexpected AI changes. The tier system ensures the most important files cannot change without a human explicitly naming them. Git hooks give humans a chance to catch problems before they reach the remote.\n\n**5. The AI's knowledge of the codebase is maintained, not assumed.** The living memory in `.docs/memory/` is read at the start of every session. The upstream baseline is tracked. The AI never operates on a stale picture of the project.\n\n**6. Governance is tool-agnostic.** The same rules exist for Claude Code, Cursor AI, and GitHub Copilot, protecting the project regardless of which AI tool an individual developer uses.\n\nBefore vs After\n---------------\n\n| Before this setup | After this setup |\n| --- | --- |\n| \"Quality gate\" means different things to different people | Named signals, all automated, all non-negotiable — agreed and documented before a line of code was written |\n| AI can write to `develop` or `main` if prompted | Branch-lock hook physically prevents any Claude Code write to `develop` or `main` |\n| QA tests are written by whoever writes them | Lead QA validates every scenario in plain English before a single Playwright line is generated |\n| Governance depends on developers reading the README | Governance enforced by hooks, CODEOWNERS, CI gates, and tool-level settings |\n| New joiners discover how things work mid-sprint | The onboarding skill guides them through setup and rules in a single session, role-aware |\n| AI tools make architectural decisions when prompts are under-specified | Scope-check and feature-spec skills surface blast radius and tier impact before implementation |\n| Release notes assembled manually under pressure | The changelog skill parses conventional commits into a four-audience changelog |\n| SIT testing can block Digital release timelines | SIT is an auto-deployed parallel lane that never gates Digital |\n| Each developer prompts AI differently for the same task | Shared skills give every developer the same approved workflow |\n\nSummary: What This Project Demonstrates\n---------------------------------------\n\nThis setup demonstrates that AI-assisted development, done well, is not about removing humans from the process. It is about using AI where it adds speed without adding risk (implementing agreed plans, generating boilerplate, drafting docs, running repetitive checks), keeping humans at every decision that matters (architecture, quality standards, test validation, release approval, tier changes), making the human-AI boundary explicit — encoded in tooling, hooks, governance files, and documented skills rather than left to individual developer judgement — and building a system that scales, where shared skills mean any developer gets the same outcome from the same workflow regardless of AI experience level.\n\nThe pre-development phase of this project is itself a demonstration of the model: a human and AI working together, with the human directing every decision of consequence and AI accelerating the execution.\n\n*Document prepared: May 2026. For questions: Squad Leads, Integration Lead, or the [Claude Code CLI Developer Onboarding Guide](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304775192) (06 User Guides → Claude Guides).*",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

