    "content": {
      "value": "This is the canonical reference for every skill in this folder. If you're looking for *why*\nthe skill system exists (the human-in-the-lead design philosophy), see Human-in-the-Lead AI Development — Project Setup Overview —\nthat page covers the rationale; this file covers the *mechanics*: what each skill does, what\nto pass it, and what it hands back.\n\nHow skills work\n---------------\n\n* Invoke a skill by typing `/skill-name` (optionally followed by an argument) in a Claude\n  Code session. Each skill is a structured, reviewable process defined in its own\n  `<skill-name>/SKILL.md` file — not a freeform prompt.\n* Where a skill takes an argument, its `SKILL.md` uses `$ARGUMENTS` to capture whatever\n  follows the skill name, e.g. `/new-component productBadge` or `/audit-tier overrides/app/hooks/use-wish-list.ts`.\n  If you invoke a skill with no argument and one is expected, Claude asks for it.\n* Every skill is **human-in-the-lead**: it either stops for confirmation at a defined\n  checkpoint, or produces a read-only report/plan rather than making changes outright.\n  See the checkpoint column in each table below.\n* These `SKILL.md` files are also the master source for `.cursor/rules/skills/*.mdc` and\n  `.vscode/instructions/workflows.md` — see [scripts/syncCursorRules.mjs](../../scripts/syncCursorRules.mjs).\n  Edit the `SKILL.md` here, then run `npm run sync:ai-rules:fix`; never edit the generated\n  Cursor/VS Code files directly.\n\nSetup & onboarding\n------------------\n\n| Skill | Usage | What it does | Example on this project | Human-in-the-lead checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [/onboarding](onboarding/SKILL.md) | `/onboarding` | Interactive walkthrough for a new developer or QA engineer — role and squad questions, mechanical setup checks (git hooks, Node version, branch), then role-specific orientation to the rules and tools. Run on a **first session on a new machine or first day on the project**. | A new Squad B (Product) developer's first session: confirms Node version, checks out `develop`, then walks through the Product-owned files in `protectedFiles.md` (`product-tile`, `display-price`, `swatch-group`). | Confirms understanding at each phase before moving on |\n| [/session-start](session-start/SKILL.md) | `/session-start` | Confirms the current branch is safe to work on, reads `.docs/memory/` tracking docs, runs health checks, and states current project position. Run at the **start of every session**. | Run first thing on `fix/checkout-address-validation` — confirms you're not on `develop`/`main`, then surfaces open items like the Global-e checkout ownership decision from `projectState.md`. | Waits for you to confirm direction before writing any code |\n| [/project-init](project-init/SKILL.md) | `/project-init` | One-time project setup walkthrough — runs `scripts/projectInitCheck.sh`, fixes automatable failures, then asks one question at a time for items that need a human answer (Jira prefix, BrowserStack, secrets, Zephyr product, etc.), updating the [7.1c CI/CD & Environment Setup Checklist](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/305430530) Confluence page (via the Atlassian MCP tools) as it goes. | Setting up a fresh clone: confirms the `ZIM` Jira prefix, BrowserStack creds for cross-browser Playwright runs, and the Zephyr test-management project key. | Asks one question at a time, waits for the answer before the next |\n\nPlanning & safety\n-----------------\n\n| Skill | Usage | What it does | Example on this project | Human-in-the-lead checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [/feature-spec](feature-spec/SKILL.md) | `/feature-spec <plain-English feature description>` | Turns a feature request into a scoped implementation spec **before any code is written** — clarifies requirements one question at a time, researches the codebase in parallel, checks sprint scope, then outputs a spec with file list, tier flags, and a ready-to-paste Claude Code prompt. | `/feature-spec Add a size-guide modal launched from the PDP image gallery` — checks `.docs/prd/` for an existing size-guide requirement and `sprintScope.md` before proposing files under `overrides/app/components/`. | Produces a plan only — you review the spec before implementation begins |\n| [/scope-check](scope-check/SKILL.md) | `/scope-check <a proposed Claude Code prompt>` | Reviews a prompt *before you run it* — classifies every file it would likely touch by protection tier, flags drift risk (refactoring upward, cross-squad reach), and verdicts it SAFE / RISKY / BLOCKED. Rewrites risky prompts into safely-scoped ones. | `/scope-check \"add a wishlist heart icon to the product tile\"` — flags `product-tile/` as Tier 2 (Squad B, notify Squad A) and verdicts RISKY until scoped to a single component. | You see the blast radius and verdict before the risky prompt is ever run |\n| [/audit-tier](audit-tier/SKILL.md) | `/audit-tier <file or directory path>` | Identifies the protection tier (1–4), owner, and import blast radius of a path under `overrides/`. Use before editing anything you're unsure about. | `/audit-tier overrides/app/hooks/use-current-basket.ts` — returns Tier 1, owner Integration/SI Partner, and lists every component that consumes basket state before you touch checkout logic. | Tells you who to notify before you touch the file |\n\nDevelopment\n-----------\n\n| Skill | Usage | What it does | Example on this project | Human-in-the-lead checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [/new-component](new-component/SKILL.md) | `/new-component <componentName>` | Scaffolds a new Zimmermann component under `overrides/app/components/` — TypeScript, Chakra-first, folder layout and props typing per `.claude/rules/conventions.md`, paired Storybook story, and a test file when the component owns real logic (branching, prop→JSX mapping decisions, event wiring) — required even if that logic's effects are also covered elsewhere (a hook's own test, another component's story), per `testing.md`. Skipped only when there's no branching/decision logic to test. | `/new-component sizeGuideModal` — scaffolds `overrides/app/components/sizeGuideModal/` with `index.tsx`, `index.stories.tsx` under `Forms/`, and a `SizeGuideModalProps` type. | You name the component and confirm the resulting file structure |\n\nQuality & review\n----------------\n\n| Skill | Usage | What it does | Example on this project | Human-in-the-lead checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [/code-quality](code-quality/SKILL.md) | `/code-quality [file or directory]` (no argument = files changed on the current branch) | **Read-only** audit against `.claude/rules/conventions.md` — naming/structure, circular imports, TypeScript coverage, test/story existence. Produces a prioritised 🔴/🟡/🔵 report. Use after implementing a feature or before opening a PR. | `/code-quality overrides/app/components/promo-code` — flags a missing `.stories.tsx` and a hardcoded hex colour instead of a theme token. | Report only — you decide what to fix and when |\n| [/pr-checklist](pr-checklist/SKILL.md) | `/pr-checklist` | Runs `/code-quality` on the current branch's changed files, classifies each by tier and reviewer requirement, and generates a copy-paste-ready PR description with a test checklist. Pairs with `/code-quality`. | On `fix/order-summary-tax-display`: generates a PR description noting `order-summary/` is Tier 2 (Squad A owner) so a non-author Squad A review is required before merge. | You review and submit the generated PR description — nothing is posted for you |\n\nQA / testing\n------------\n\n| Skill | Usage | What it does | Example on this project | Human-in-the-lead checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [/qa-generate](qa-generate/SKILL.md) | `/qa-generate` (then answers Jira story questions) | Two-step, human-gated E2E test generation: drafts a Gherkin `.feature` file and **stops completely**; only generates the Playwright spec after Lead QA has explicitly validated (and optionally annotated) the feature file. Use for any new storefront user journey needing E2E coverage. | `/qa-generate` against `ZIM-142` (Guest checkout on the AU site) — drafts `e2e/features/ZIM-142-guest-checkout.feature` across the AU/US/UK/EU/HK/ME site fixtures for Lead QA sign-off before any Playwright code exists. | Playwright spec is not generated until Lead QA signs off on the `.feature` file |\n\nContentful\n----------\n\n| Skill | Usage | What it does | Example on this project | Human-in-the-lead checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [/contentful-register-component](contentful-register-component/SKILL.md) | `/contentful-register-component <component file path>` | Reads an existing component's TypeScript props, generates a `*ContentfulDefinition.ts` file mapping each prop to a Contentful variable type, and wires it into the `defineComponents` registry. | `/contentful-register-component overrides/app/components/heroBanner/index.tsx` — maps `headline`, `ctaLabel`, and `backgroundImage` props to Contentful Studio variable types so merchandising can build homepage banners without a code change. | You review the generated definition (and any skipped/ambiguous props) before it's published in Contentful |\n| [/contentful-drift-check](contentful-drift-check/SKILL.md) | `/contentful-drift-check [component or directory path]` (no argument = scan all registered components) | **Read-only** comparison of component props against their paired Contentful definitions — reports added props, removed props, and type mismatches. Use after props change, or to audit everything at once. | `/contentful-drift-check overrides/app/components/productTile` — catches a newly added `showQuickAdd` prop that isn't yet exposed in the Contentful definition, before merchandising hits a silently-ignored field. | Report only — you resolve any drift found |\n\nRelease management\n------------------\n\n| Skill | Usage | What it does | Example on this project | Human-in-the-lead checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [/generate-changelog](generate-changelog/SKILL.md) | `/generate-changelog` (then answers version/range questions) | Builds a structured, four-audience changelog (dev team, QA, product, GitHub Release) from conventional commits and the upstream baseline. Writes to `docs/releases/`. Use before cutting a release branch. | Run before cutting `release/2.4.0` — produces a QA-facing summary calling out the new gift-card redemption flow so SIT can prioritise those test cases. | You review the changelog before it's published; the GitHub Release command is proposed, never run automatically |\n| [/upstream-sync-review](upstream-sync-review/SKILL.md) | `/upstream-sync-review` (then supply a candidate `@salesforce/retail-react-app` version) | Reviews upstream release notes against `.docs/memory/upstreamBaseline.md`, maps changes to our override surface, and recommends BUMP / DEFER / WAIT — per the \"read the changelog, bump opportunistically\" policy in `.docs/upstreamPolicy.md`. | `/upstream-sync-review 10.2.0` — checks whether the upstream `product-tile` changes conflict with our Tier 2 override before recommending BUMP / DEFER / WAIT. | Recommendation only — you approve and run the actual `npm install` bump |\n\nAdding a new skill\n------------------\n\n1. Create `.claude/skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md` with `name` and `description` frontmatter (see any existing skill for the format).\n2. Add it to the appropriate table above.\n3. Add it to the skill list in [scripts/syncCursorRules.mjs](../../scripts/syncCursorRules.mjs) so it gets mirrored to `.cursor/rules/skills/` and `.vscode/instructions/workflows.md`, then run `npm run sync:ai-rules:fix`.\n4. If it belongs in the [Project Setup Overview](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/305102849) Confluence page's \"Part 2\" narrative, add a one-line mention there — but keep the detailed reference here to avoid duplication.\n\n---\n\n*Source:* `.claude/skills/README.md` *in the zimmermann-pwa repository, with SFCC-project-specific examples added for the Confluence reference page.*",
